> Well I think I found what I was missing:
> 
> Which platforms/operating systems are supported by Rserve?
> 
> Rserve should work on any plarform supporting shared libraries which is also supported by R. The following platforms were tested and are known to work:
> 	• Linux (PPC, x86)
> 	• Windows (x86)
> 	• Mac OS X / Darwin (PPC, x86)
> 	• AIX (PPC since version 0.1-10)
> 	• SunOS (sun4u since version 0.2)
> 	• HP-UX (HP-9000 since version 0.3-15)
> All platforms except Windows support parallel connections and local (unix) sockets.
> From Rserve's FAQ. Shame on me. Is this still the case at the time being?
> 
> If yes, which I suppose so, what would be a workaround? Starting as many Rserve (non-daemonized) processes as parallel task I want to perform? Is there a better way?
> 

Not really. Simply put, you have to start a separate process for each concurrent connection. There was once a patch to simulate fork() by creating a new process and re-initializing at the expense of startup-up for each connection, but since it broke other things and any serious deployment will use unix it was never incorporated in the main trunk. Probably the best bet on Windows would be to spawn a pool of workers for warm startup-up and then add workers from new processes if the load increases. Again, since this is far inferior to any unix-based solution, I have not seen anyone willing to devote resources to such a project, though (especially these days when you can get unix computing VMs so easily). 

Cheers,
Simon



