> On Dec 12, 2014, at 6:54 AM, Antje Janosch <antje.janosch at web.de> wrote:
> 
> Hi Simon,
> 
> theoretically, it would make sense to push each vector at a time. But then it gets very very slow (as I have to iterate over the whole table for each vector). 
> So, I decided to read in a certain number of vectors (let's say 10) and push this as list to R. Then, I would read in the next 10 vectors, push it as another list to R and combine them into a single list. With this method, I would save memory and I could speed up a bit.

I'm not sure how this speeds up anything - since you have to go from row-oiriented access to columns, you have to alway fill all columns, otherwise you'd have to run multiple iterations through the dataset. The fastest is simply to build the entire DF and push it.


> But it also means that for each table row iteration, I have to store its 10 values in 10 different data vectors.
> I was wondering if there is a way to set these values as single entries directly to the initialized RList (rList.put(column, new REXPDouble(nRows))). But I cannot find a way to alter a single value then.
> Do I need to store my data within a temporary vector (let's say double[] ) to then add it to the RList? Or is there any other way?
> 

Note that REXPDouble() is just a wrapper around double[] so there is no copying involved, you can modify any value of the vector. 

double xd[] = new double[n];
REXPDouble x = REXPDouble(xd);

You can modify the vector directly with xd[i] = d;
I was assuming that's what you do - allocate the data, convert in on the Java side from row to column format by filling the columns, then create RList from it and call createDataFrame()

Cheers,
Simon



